1
00:00:00,000 --> 00:00:02,204
People love to talk about the fifties

2
00:00:02,204 --> 00:00:03,157
and the sixties.

3
00:00:03,337 --> 00:00:04,347
But the seventies?

4
00:00:04,527 --> 00:00:06,189
They often get skipped,

5
00:00:06,189 --> 00:00:09,005
or remembered only for bad haircuts and

6
00:00:09,005 --> 00:00:09,439
disco.

7
00:00:09,619 --> 00:00:11,090
Yet if you were a kid in

8
00:00:11,090 --> 00:00:13,481
nineteen seventy-two, you know it was a

9
00:00:13,481 --> 00:00:15,075
wonderful time to grow up.

10
00:00:15,255 --> 00:00:17,561
It was a decade of freedom,

11
00:00:17,561 --> 00:00:21,575
Big Wheels, Saturday morning cartoons and long,

12
00:00:21,575 --> 00:00:22,686
lazy summers.

13
00:00:23,036 --> 00:00:24,623
In nineteen seventy-two,

14
00:00:24,623 --> 00:00:27,072
the oldest baby boomers were in their

15
00:00:27,072 --> 00:00:29,851
mid-twenties, and the youngest were eight.

16
00:00:30,031 --> 00:00:32,279
A new generation of kids was right

17
00:00:32,279 --> 00:00:33,072
behind them.

18
00:00:33,252 --> 00:00:35,088
Families were a little smaller,

19
00:00:35,088 --> 00:00:36,746
houses were a little bigger,

20
00:00:36,746 --> 00:00:38,819
and the country was going through a

21
00:00:38,819 --> 00:00:39,708
lot of changes.

22
00:00:39,888 --> 00:00:40,887
But for a kid,

23
00:00:40,887 --> 00:00:43,385
life was mostly about the next bike

24
00:00:43,385 --> 00:00:43,742
ride.

25
00:00:44,092 --> 00:00:45,624
So let's go back.

26
00:00:45,804 --> 00:00:47,991
Let's spend a year growing up in

27
00:00:47,991 --> 00:00:49,972
nineteen seventy-two America.

28
00:00:50,152 --> 00:00:52,338
Home, school, television,

29
00:00:52,338 --> 00:00:54,961
toys, cars and the big news of

30
00:00:54,961 --> 00:00:55,748
the year.

31
00:00:55,928 --> 00:00:56,951
If you were there,

32
00:00:56,951 --> 00:00:58,883
I think it's going to come rushing

33
00:00:58,883 --> 00:00:59,167
back.

34
00:00:59,518 --> 00:01:01,976
The house in nineteen seventy-two had a

35
00:01:01,976 --> 00:01:03,047
look all its own.

36
00:01:03,227 --> 00:01:05,085
Wood paneling on the walls.

37
00:01:05,265 --> 00:01:07,860
Shag carpet in harvest gold,

38
00:01:07,860 --> 00:01:10,640
avocado green or burnt orange.

39
00:01:10,820 --> 00:01:13,495
A kitchen with appliances in the same

40
00:01:13,495 --> 00:01:14,001
colors.

41
00:01:14,181 --> 00:01:16,384
A big console television,

42
00:01:16,384 --> 00:01:18,059
now often in color.

43
00:01:18,239 --> 00:01:20,758
And macrame plant hangers in the window.

44
00:01:21,109 --> 00:01:22,941
In nineteen seventy-two,

45
00:01:22,941 --> 00:01:25,536
about half of American homes had a

46
00:01:25,536 --> 00:01:28,819
color television, and the share was growing

47
00:01:28,819 --> 00:01:29,201
fast.

48
00:01:29,381 --> 00:01:32,294
Families still had just a few channels,

49
00:01:32,294 --> 00:01:35,133
usually the three networks and maybe a

50
00:01:35,133 --> 00:01:37,972
public station or a local independent.

51
00:01:38,152 --> 00:01:40,953
And there was still only one television

52
00:01:40,953 --> 00:01:42,030
in most houses,

53
00:01:42,030 --> 00:01:44,329
which led to a lot of arguments.

54
00:01:44,679 --> 00:01:47,497
More mothers were working outside the home

55
00:01:47,497 --> 00:01:48,638
than ever before.

56
00:01:48,818 --> 00:01:50,710
By nineteen seventy-two,

57
00:01:50,710 --> 00:01:53,627
around four in ten married women held

58
00:01:53,627 --> 00:01:54,100
a job.

59
00:01:54,280 --> 00:01:56,511
That meant more kids came home from

60
00:01:56,511 --> 00:01:58,104
school to an empty house,

61
00:01:58,104 --> 00:01:59,953
with a key on a string around

62
00:01:59,953 --> 00:02:00,654
their neck.

63
00:02:00,834 --> 00:02:02,808
They called them latchkey kids,

64
00:02:02,808 --> 00:02:04,974
and they learned to make their own

65
00:02:04,974 --> 00:02:05,420
snacks.

66
00:02:05,770 --> 00:02:08,143
The family dinner was still a big

67
00:02:08,143 --> 00:02:09,078
deal, though.

68
00:02:09,258 --> 00:02:12,236
Hamburger Helper had just arrived in nineteen

69
00:02:12,236 --> 00:02:15,281
seventy-one, and it quickly became a weeknight

70
00:02:15,281 --> 00:02:15,876
favorite.

71
00:02:16,056 --> 00:02:18,532
Mothers made tuna casserole,

72
00:02:18,532 --> 00:02:21,803
sloppy joes, fish sticks and Shake 'n

73
00:02:21,803 --> 00:02:22,952
Bake chicken.

74
00:02:23,132 --> 00:02:24,249
And for dessert,

75
00:02:24,249 --> 00:02:26,691
there might be Jell-O with fruit in

76
00:02:26,691 --> 00:02:28,505
it, or a slice of Sara Lee

77
00:02:28,505 --> 00:02:30,040
cake from the freezer.

78
00:02:30,391 --> 00:02:33,029
And kids in seventy-two spent most of

79
00:02:33,029 --> 00:02:34,384
their time outside.

80
00:02:34,564 --> 00:02:36,359
Mothers sent them out in the morning

81
00:02:36,359 --> 00:02:37,955
and called them home for dinner.

82
00:02:38,135 --> 00:02:40,526
Nobody knew exactly where they were.

83
00:02:40,706 --> 00:02:43,088
They rode bikes miles from home,

84
00:02:43,088 --> 00:02:45,024
explored creeks and woods,

85
00:02:45,024 --> 00:02:47,779
built forts, and played ball in empty

86
00:02:47,779 --> 00:02:50,459
lots until the streetlights came on.

87
00:02:50,809 --> 00:02:52,922
The bike of the moment was the

88
00:02:52,922 --> 00:02:55,740
Schwinn Sting-Ray, with its banana seat,

89
00:02:55,740 --> 00:02:58,487
high-rise handlebars and a sissy bar in

90
00:02:58,487 --> 00:02:59,121
the back.

91
00:02:59,301 --> 00:03:01,446
Some had a stick shift on the

92
00:03:01,446 --> 00:03:03,295
frame, like a sports car.

93
00:03:03,475 --> 00:03:06,167
Kids put baseball cards in the spokes

94
00:03:06,167 --> 00:03:09,440
with clothespins, so their bikes sounded like

95
00:03:09,440 --> 00:03:10,313
motorcycles.

96
00:03:10,664 --> 00:03:13,090
Younger kids had the Big Wheel,

97
00:03:13,090 --> 00:03:15,830
a low plastic tricycle with a giant

98
00:03:15,830 --> 00:03:18,257
front wheel, made by Marx Toys.

99
00:03:18,437 --> 00:03:20,192
On a sidewalk or driveway,

100
00:03:20,192 --> 00:03:22,352
a Big Wheel made a roaring sound

101
00:03:22,352 --> 00:03:25,052
that every parent of the seventies still

102
00:03:25,052 --> 00:03:25,728
remembers.

103
00:03:25,908 --> 00:03:27,048
Kids raced them,

104
00:03:27,048 --> 00:03:29,472
crashed them, and spun them out in

105
00:03:29,472 --> 00:03:30,470
perfect skids.

106
00:03:30,821 --> 00:03:31,780
In the summer,

107
00:03:31,780 --> 00:03:33,700
kids ran through sprinklers,

108
00:03:33,700 --> 00:03:35,413
played on Slip 'N Slides,

109
00:03:35,413 --> 00:03:37,744
and spent whole days at the public

110
00:03:37,744 --> 00:03:38,704
swimming pool.

111
00:03:38,884 --> 00:03:40,741
They drank from the garden hose.

112
00:03:40,921 --> 00:03:43,347
Ice cream trucks came down the street

113
00:03:43,347 --> 00:03:44,265
playing music.

114
00:03:44,445 --> 00:03:46,027
And if you had a quarter,

115
00:03:46,027 --> 00:03:47,736
you could get a Bomb Pop or

116
00:03:47,736 --> 00:03:48,369
a Push-Up.

117
00:03:48,720 --> 00:03:50,949
Kids also had a lot of jobs.

118
00:03:51,129 --> 00:03:53,450
Paper routes, mowing lawns,

119
00:03:53,450 --> 00:03:57,061
babysitting, and shoveling snow in winter.

120
00:03:57,241 --> 00:04:00,650
A paperboy in nineteen seventy-two might earn

121
00:04:00,650 --> 00:04:02,923
ten or fifteen dollars a week,

122
00:04:02,923 --> 00:04:04,892
which felt like a fortune.

123
00:04:05,072 --> 00:04:07,539
He collected from each house on Friday,

124
00:04:07,539 --> 00:04:10,511
and learned exactly which neighbors tipped well

125
00:04:10,511 --> 00:04:11,586
and which didn't.

126
00:04:11,936 --> 00:04:14,745
School in seventy-two was changing.

127
00:04:14,925 --> 00:04:17,764
Many schools had new open classrooms,

128
00:04:17,764 --> 00:04:19,836
with no walls between them.

129
00:04:20,016 --> 00:04:21,990
Students used workbooks,

130
00:04:21,990 --> 00:04:24,869
filmstrips and overhead projectors.

131
00:04:25,049 --> 00:04:27,433
And many schools still smelled of the

132
00:04:27,433 --> 00:04:29,624
purple ink from the ditto machine.

133
00:04:29,804 --> 00:04:32,520
A fresh, damp ditto sheet was something

134
00:04:32,520 --> 00:04:34,889
kids would hold up to their noses.

135
00:04:35,239 --> 00:04:38,038
Kids carried metal lunch boxes with their

136
00:04:38,038 --> 00:04:39,813
favorite TV shows on them.

137
00:04:39,993 --> 00:04:41,534
The Partridge Family,

138
00:04:41,534 --> 00:04:42,708
The Brady Bunch,

139
00:04:42,708 --> 00:04:45,496
Scooby-Doo, Star Trek and The Waltons.

140
00:04:45,676 --> 00:04:49,417
At lunch, they traded snacks like Twinkies,

141
00:04:49,417 --> 00:04:52,375
Ding Dongs and Hostess fruit pies.

142
00:04:52,555 --> 00:04:54,958
And they drank milk from little cartons.

143
00:04:55,309 --> 00:04:58,008
And in June of nineteen seventy-two,

144
00:04:58,008 --> 00:05:00,333
a new law called Title Nine was

145
00:05:00,333 --> 00:05:00,858
signed.

146
00:05:01,038 --> 00:05:03,024
It said that no one could be

147
00:05:03,024 --> 00:05:06,288
excluded from any school program that received

148
00:05:06,288 --> 00:05:08,770
federal money because of their sex.

149
00:05:08,950 --> 00:05:10,210
Within a few years,

150
00:05:10,210 --> 00:05:12,796
the number of girls playing high school

151
00:05:12,796 --> 00:05:13,989
sports multiplied.

152
00:05:14,169 --> 00:05:16,356
Many girls of the seventies were the

153
00:05:16,356 --> 00:05:18,421
first in their families to play on

154
00:05:18,421 --> 00:05:19,150
a real team.

155
00:05:19,500 --> 00:05:22,364
Saturday morning was the most important time

156
00:05:22,364 --> 00:05:23,145
of the week.

157
00:05:23,325 --> 00:05:24,853
Kids woke up early,

158
00:05:24,853 --> 00:05:27,828
poured a giant bowl of sugary cereal,

159
00:05:27,828 --> 00:05:30,159
and sat on the floor in front

160
00:05:30,159 --> 00:05:32,973
of the television in their pajamas.

161
00:05:33,153 --> 00:05:35,102
Cap'n Crunch, Froot Loops,

162
00:05:35,102 --> 00:05:37,426
Lucky Charms and Count Chocula.

163
00:05:37,606 --> 00:05:40,404
Cartoons ran from seven until noon.

164
00:05:40,754 --> 00:05:42,836
In nineteen seventy-two,

165
00:05:42,836 --> 00:05:44,919
kids watched Scooby-Doo,

166
00:05:44,919 --> 00:05:47,522
Fat Albert and the Cosby Kids,

167
00:05:47,522 --> 00:05:49,258
The Bugs Bunny Show,

168
00:05:49,258 --> 00:05:50,994
and The Flintstones.

169
00:05:51,174 --> 00:05:54,317
And there were short educational cartoons between

170
00:05:54,317 --> 00:05:54,959
the shows.

171
00:05:55,139 --> 00:05:57,974
Many grown-ups today can still sing every

172
00:05:57,974 --> 00:05:59,703
word of Schoolhouse Rock,

173
00:05:59,703 --> 00:06:03,161
which first aired in early nineteen seventy-three.

174
00:06:03,511 --> 00:06:05,644
By noon, the cartoons ended,

175
00:06:05,644 --> 00:06:07,702
and kids were sent outside.

176
00:06:07,882 --> 00:06:08,915
That was the deal.

177
00:06:09,095 --> 00:06:11,738
Parents could sleep in on Saturday morning,

178
00:06:11,738 --> 00:06:13,704
and the kids got their cartoons.

179
00:06:13,884 --> 00:06:16,415
Then the afternoon belonged to the neighborhood.

180
00:06:16,766 --> 00:06:19,448
Saturday afternoons might mean a matinee at

181
00:06:19,448 --> 00:06:20,945
the local movie theater.

182
00:06:21,125 --> 00:06:22,871
In nineteen seventy-two,

183
00:06:22,871 --> 00:06:25,343
the big movies were The Godfather,

184
00:06:25,343 --> 00:06:28,180
which most kids weren't allowed to see,

185
00:06:28,180 --> 00:06:30,144
and The Poseidon Adventure,

186
00:06:30,144 --> 00:06:31,307
which they were.

187
00:06:31,487 --> 00:06:33,747
Disney rereleased its classics,

188
00:06:33,747 --> 00:06:35,133
and kids saw Bambi,

189
00:06:35,133 --> 00:06:37,611
Lady and the Tramp or Pinocchio in

190
00:06:37,611 --> 00:06:40,090
a real theater for about a dollar.

191
00:06:40,440 --> 00:06:43,992
Evening television in nineteen seventy-two was some

192
00:06:43,992 --> 00:06:45,525
of the best ever made.

193
00:06:45,705 --> 00:06:48,597
All in the Family was the most-watched

194
00:06:48,597 --> 00:06:49,815
show in America.

195
00:06:49,995 --> 00:06:52,386
Sanford and Son was a new hit.

196
00:06:52,566 --> 00:06:53,702
And in the fall,

197
00:06:53,702 --> 00:06:56,327
two new shows started that would last

198
00:06:56,327 --> 00:06:57,036
for years.

199
00:06:57,216 --> 00:07:02,371
M*A*S*H and The Waltons.

200
00:07:02,721 --> 00:07:05,616
Families watched The Mary Tyler Moore Show,

201
00:07:05,616 --> 00:07:08,310
The Carol Burnett Show and The Partridge

202
00:07:08,310 --> 00:07:08,781
Family.

203
00:07:08,961 --> 00:07:10,914
Kids were allowed to stay up for

204
00:07:10,914 --> 00:07:13,294
The Wonderful World of Disney on Sunday

205
00:07:13,294 --> 00:07:13,721
nights.

206
00:07:13,901 --> 00:07:15,877
And on weekday afternoons,

207
00:07:15,877 --> 00:07:18,080
a new version of The Price Is

208
00:07:18,080 --> 00:07:20,208
Right, hosted by Bob Barker,

209
00:07:20,208 --> 00:07:21,575
started that fall.

210
00:07:21,755 --> 00:07:23,590
It would run for decades.

211
00:07:23,940 --> 00:07:26,821
And in November of nineteen seventy-two,

212
00:07:26,821 --> 00:07:29,486
something new appeared in a few homes

213
00:07:29,486 --> 00:07:30,639
in Pennsylvania.

214
00:07:30,819 --> 00:07:33,675
A cable channel called Home Box Office.

215
00:07:33,855 --> 00:07:34,714
HBO.

216
00:07:34,894 --> 00:07:35,832
At the time,

217
00:07:35,832 --> 00:07:37,553
almost nobody noticed.

218
00:07:37,733 --> 00:07:40,739
It would one day change television completely.

219
00:07:41,090 --> 00:07:43,737
The toys of seventy-two were wonderful.

220
00:07:43,917 --> 00:07:48,162
Lite-Brite, Spirograph, Etch A Sketch and Lincoln

221
00:07:48,162 --> 00:07:48,596
Logs.

222
00:07:48,776 --> 00:07:52,098
Evel Knievel action figures were coming soon,

223
00:07:52,098 --> 00:07:55,051
and Hot Wheels raced down orange plastic

224
00:07:55,051 --> 00:07:55,567
tracks.

225
00:07:55,747 --> 00:07:58,027
Girls had Barbie and Holly Hobbie,

226
00:07:58,027 --> 00:07:59,569
and the Easy-Bake Oven,

227
00:07:59,569 --> 00:08:01,581
which baked a tiny cake with a

228
00:08:01,581 --> 00:08:02,319
light bulb.

229
00:08:02,669 --> 00:08:04,498
And in nineteen seventy-two,

230
00:08:04,498 --> 00:08:07,176
two things appeared that would change the

231
00:08:07,176 --> 00:08:07,568
world.

232
00:08:07,748 --> 00:08:09,318
The Magnavox Odyssey,

233
00:08:09,318 --> 00:08:11,859
the first home video game console,

234
00:08:11,859 --> 00:08:14,401
which plugged into the television.

235
00:08:14,581 --> 00:08:17,492
And Pong, the arcade game made by

236
00:08:17,492 --> 00:08:19,875
a new company called Atari.

237
00:08:20,055 --> 00:08:22,470
Two paddles, one bouncing dot.

238
00:08:22,650 --> 00:08:23,830
It sounds simple,

239
00:08:23,830 --> 00:08:25,842
but kids lined up to play it.

240
00:08:26,193 --> 00:08:28,445
Kids also collected things.

241
00:08:28,625 --> 00:08:31,736
Baseball cards, with a flat pink stick

242
00:08:31,736 --> 00:08:33,455
of gum in every pack.

243
00:08:33,635 --> 00:08:37,109
Wacky Packages stickers that made fun of

244
00:08:37,109 --> 00:08:38,325
real products.

245
00:08:38,505 --> 00:08:41,228
Comic books from the spinning rack at

246
00:08:41,228 --> 00:08:43,950
the drugstore, for twenty cents each.

247
00:08:44,130 --> 00:08:46,847
And many kids kept their collections in

248
00:08:46,847 --> 00:08:48,519
shoeboxes under the bed,

249
00:08:48,519 --> 00:08:50,957
where many of them stayed for fifty

250
00:08:50,957 --> 00:08:51,375
years.

251
00:08:51,725 --> 00:08:54,926
The family car of nineteen seventy-two was

252
00:08:54,926 --> 00:08:55,231
big.

253
00:08:55,411 --> 00:08:58,282
A station wagon with fake wood paneling

254
00:08:58,282 --> 00:09:00,859
on the sides and a rear-facing back

255
00:09:00,859 --> 00:09:01,228
seat.

256
00:09:01,408 --> 00:09:03,257
A Ford Country Squire,

257
00:09:03,257 --> 00:09:06,621
a Chevy Kingswood or an Oldsmobile Vista

258
00:09:06,621 --> 00:09:07,294
Cruiser.

259
00:09:07,474 --> 00:09:10,908
Gasoline was still about thirty-six cents a

260
00:09:10,908 --> 00:09:11,468
gallon.

261
00:09:11,648 --> 00:09:13,568
Nobody worried about it,

262
00:09:13,568 --> 00:09:13,888
yet.

263
00:09:14,239 --> 00:09:16,265
Seat belts were in the cars,

264
00:09:16,265 --> 00:09:18,291
but almost nobody wore them.

265
00:09:18,471 --> 00:09:20,422
Kids rolled around in the back,

266
00:09:20,422 --> 00:09:22,436
or lay in the cargo area looking

267
00:09:22,436 --> 00:09:23,695
out the back window.

268
00:09:23,875 --> 00:09:24,904
On long trips,

269
00:09:24,904 --> 00:09:27,476
they played the license plate game,

270
00:09:27,476 --> 00:09:30,195
counted cows, and fought over who was

271
00:09:30,195 --> 00:09:31,224
touching whom.

272
00:09:31,404 --> 00:09:33,656
And there was always a cooler with

273
00:09:33,656 --> 00:09:34,980
sandwiches and soda.

274
00:09:35,330 --> 00:09:38,186
The radio played the hits of seventy-two.

275
00:09:38,366 --> 00:09:40,682
American Pie by Don McLean,

276
00:09:40,682 --> 00:09:43,428
which was number one in January.

277
00:09:43,608 --> 00:09:45,651
Lean on Me by Bill Withers.

278
00:09:45,831 --> 00:09:48,792
Alone Again by Gilbert O'Sullivan.

279
00:09:48,972 --> 00:09:50,876
I Can See Clearly Now,

280
00:09:50,876 --> 00:09:52,087
Brand New Key,

281
00:09:52,087 --> 00:09:53,732
and Song Sung Blue.

282
00:09:53,912 --> 00:09:55,897
Dad usually picked the station.

283
00:09:56,247 --> 00:09:59,554
Nineteen seventy-two was full of big news,

284
00:09:59,554 --> 00:10:01,995
much of which went over a kid's

285
00:10:01,995 --> 00:10:02,388
head.

286
00:10:02,568 --> 00:10:05,964
In February, President Nixon traveled to China,

287
00:10:05,964 --> 00:10:09,070
the first American president ever to visit.

288
00:10:09,250 --> 00:10:12,030
Families watched pictures of him walking on

289
00:10:12,030 --> 00:10:13,000
the Great Wall.

290
00:10:13,180 --> 00:10:15,777
It seemed impossible just a few years

291
00:10:15,777 --> 00:10:16,338
earlier.

292
00:10:16,688 --> 00:10:19,289
In June, a small burglary happened at

293
00:10:19,289 --> 00:10:22,312
an office building in Washington called the

294
00:10:22,312 --> 00:10:23,015
Watergate.

295
00:10:23,195 --> 00:10:25,507
Most people paid little attention at the

296
00:10:25,507 --> 00:10:25,796
time.

297
00:10:25,976 --> 00:10:28,338
But it would grow into the biggest

298
00:10:28,338 --> 00:10:30,978
political scandal in American history,

299
00:10:30,978 --> 00:10:32,367
and two years later,

300
00:10:32,367 --> 00:10:34,381
President Nixon would resign.

301
00:10:34,561 --> 00:10:37,448
Kids of seventy-two would watch the hearings

302
00:10:37,448 --> 00:10:38,892
on TV the next summer.

303
00:10:39,242 --> 00:10:42,295
That summer, swimmer Mark Spitz won seven

304
00:10:42,295 --> 00:10:44,380
gold medals at the Olympics,

305
00:10:44,380 --> 00:10:46,838
and his poster went up on bedroom

306
00:10:46,838 --> 00:10:48,402
walls across America.

307
00:10:48,582 --> 00:10:51,374
The war in Vietnam was winding down,

308
00:10:51,374 --> 00:10:54,475
and most American ground troops had come

309
00:10:54,475 --> 00:10:54,863
home.

310
00:10:55,043 --> 00:10:56,340
And in December,

311
00:10:56,340 --> 00:10:59,095
Apollo seventeen flew to the moon.

312
00:10:59,275 --> 00:11:01,276
Nobody knew it would be the last

313
00:11:01,276 --> 00:11:02,839
time humans walked there.

314
00:11:03,189 --> 00:11:05,674
The seventies were also a time of

315
00:11:05,674 --> 00:11:06,126
worry.

316
00:11:06,306 --> 00:11:07,781
Cities were struggling.

317
00:11:07,961 --> 00:11:10,777
Factories were starting to close in some

318
00:11:10,777 --> 00:11:11,269
places.

319
00:11:11,449 --> 00:11:13,063
Prices were creeping up.

320
00:11:13,243 --> 00:11:16,378
And pollution had become a serious concern.

321
00:11:16,558 --> 00:11:20,184
The Environmental Protection Agency had been created

322
00:11:20,184 --> 00:11:21,579
in nineteen seventy,

323
00:11:21,579 --> 00:11:23,880
and the first Earth Day that same

324
00:11:23,880 --> 00:11:26,320
year had made kids aware of litter,

325
00:11:26,320 --> 00:11:27,854
smog and dirty rivers.

326
00:11:28,205 --> 00:11:30,870
A famous television ad showed a Native

327
00:11:30,870 --> 00:11:33,536
American man shedding a single tear at

328
00:11:33,536 --> 00:11:34,869
a polluted highway.

329
00:11:35,049 --> 00:11:36,841
Keep America Beautiful,

330
00:11:36,841 --> 00:11:37,464
it said.

331
00:11:37,644 --> 00:11:39,490
Kids took it seriously.

332
00:11:39,670 --> 00:11:41,070
They picked up litter,

333
00:11:41,070 --> 00:11:42,916
collected cans for recycling,

334
00:11:42,916 --> 00:11:45,909
and lectured their parents about throwing trash

335
00:11:45,909 --> 00:11:47,309
out of the car window.

336
00:11:47,659 --> 00:11:51,110
Looking back, nineteen seventy-two was the last

337
00:11:51,110 --> 00:11:53,754
year of a certain kind of innocence.

338
00:11:53,934 --> 00:11:56,786
The next year brought the oil crisis,

339
00:11:56,786 --> 00:11:58,636
gas lines and inflation.

340
00:11:58,816 --> 00:12:00,349
Watergate unfolded.

341
00:12:00,529 --> 00:12:01,934
But in seventy-two,

342
00:12:01,934 --> 00:12:02,969
gas was cheap,

343
00:12:02,969 --> 00:12:04,892
the moon was within reach,

344
00:12:04,892 --> 00:12:06,742
and a kid on a bike could

345
00:12:06,742 --> 00:12:09,108
ride all day and nobody worried.

346
00:12:09,458 --> 00:12:11,532
That's why so many people who grew

347
00:12:11,532 --> 00:12:13,607
up in the early seventies feel the

348
00:12:13,607 --> 00:12:15,193
decade has been forgotten.

349
00:12:15,373 --> 00:12:17,306
It doesn't have a neat image like

350
00:12:17,306 --> 00:12:18,009
the fifties.

351
00:12:18,189 --> 00:12:20,321
But it was the decade when kids

352
00:12:20,321 --> 00:12:23,005
had the most freedom of any generation,

353
00:12:23,005 --> 00:12:25,963
before video games moved inside and parents

354
00:12:25,963 --> 00:12:27,477
started to worry more.

355
00:12:27,827 --> 00:12:30,436
If you grew up in nineteen seventy-two,

356
00:12:30,436 --> 00:12:31,507
you remember it.

357
00:12:31,687 --> 00:12:33,556
The smell of the ditto sheet.

358
00:12:33,736 --> 00:12:35,582
The sound of the Big Wheel.

359
00:12:35,762 --> 00:12:37,806
Saturday morning cereal.

360
00:12:37,986 --> 00:12:40,447
American Pie on the car radio.

361
00:12:40,627 --> 00:12:42,844
It wasn't a forgotten decade to you.

362
00:12:43,024 --> 00:12:44,522
It was your childhood.

363
00:12:44,872 --> 00:12:46,578
So tell us in the comments.

364
00:12:46,758 --> 00:12:49,621
How old were you in nineteen seventy-two,

365
00:12:49,621 --> 00:12:51,716
and what do you remember most?

366
00:12:51,896 --> 00:12:54,462
What was your favorite Saturday morning cartoon?

367
00:12:54,642 --> 00:12:56,035
We read every comment.

368
00:12:56,215 --> 00:12:59,250
Next, we're asking what made being ten

369
00:12:59,250 --> 00:13:02,844
years old in nineteen sixty-three so special.

370
00:13:03,024 --> 00:13:04,796
Thank you for spending this time with

371
00:13:04,796 --> 00:13:04,940
me.
